# Manuscript content. Inline markup: **bold**, *italic*, ^sup^, [@key;key] citations.
TITLE = "Are Digital Gait Biomarkers Ready to Predict Falls? A Systematic Review and PROBAST+AI Appraisal of Wearable and Vision-Based Prognostic Models in Older Adults"

ABSTRACT = [
("Background/Objectives:", " Wearable and vision-based gait analysis is widely proposed for fall-risk screening, but many studies classify past fallers or report optimistic development performance. We assessed whether gait-based models predict future falls in older adults with clinically adequate validity."),
("Methods:", " Nine databases, Google Scholar, registries, and citation sources were searched from January 2000 to 20 July 2026 (PROSPERO CRD420261502037). Eligible studies measured gait with wearable or vision sensors in adults aged ≥60 years before fall follow-up and reported individualized model performance. Overlapping reports were grouped into cohort families; risk of bias was assessed with PROBAST+AI."),
("Results:", " Forty-seven reports from 37 cohort families (15–1705 participants; horizons 7 days to 5 years) were included; 45 used wearable sensors and two ambient vision. The median representative area under the curve (AUC) was 0.76 (interquartile range 0.72–0.84; 24 families), with the highest values in small samples evaluated in their own development data. The only independent external validation yielded AUCs of 0.49–0.59. One report estimated calibration slope and intercept; none assessed clinical utility or impact. All 47 evaluations were at high risk of bias, driven by the analysis domain."),
("Conclusions:", " Gait carries a genuine prognostic signal for falls, but no wearable or vision-based model is ready for clinical deployment. Independent multisite validation of locked sensor-to-model pipelines, with calibration and net-benefit analysis, is the essential next step."),
]
KEYWORDS = "accidental falls; older adults; gait analysis; wearable sensors; computer vision; prognostic model; machine learning; external validation; PROBAST+AI; digital biomarkers"

# Body: list of (kind, payload)
BODY = [
('h1', "1. Introduction"),
('p', "Falls are a leading cause of injury, loss of independence, and injury-related death in older adults, and their frequency rises steeply with age, frailty, and multimorbidity [@who;montero]. The World Guidelines for Falls Prevention and Management recommend opportunistic case finding followed by multifactorial assessment of those at high risk, with gait and balance evaluation at its core [@montero]. In practice, however, risk stratification relies largely on fall history, clinical questions, and brief performance tests. These tools are episodic, depend on the setting and examiner, and have limited predictive accuracy when used alone [@montero]. An objective, repeatable, and scalable estimate of an individual’s probability of falling over a defined period would therefore be clinically valuable, provided that it is accurate, calibrated, and transportable to the settings in which it will be used."),
('p', "Gait is a biologically plausible source of such information because walking integrates musculoskeletal, neurological, sensory, cardiovascular, and cognitive function. Slower speed, reduced rhythm, and greater stride-to-stride variability have been prospectively associated with incident falls [@verghese]. Inertial measurement units (IMUs), pressure-sensing insoles, smartphones, and wrist devices can now quantify these features during structured tests or during days of free-living activity, and depth or video cameras can estimate gait without any body-worn device [@howcroft2013;patel;han]. Such “digital gait biomarkers” are therefore increasingly promoted as diagnostic and prognostic tools for fall-risk screening."),
('p', "Whether these technologies are ready for clinical decisions is less clear. Three analytically distinct tasks are often labelled “fall prediction”: detection of a fall as it occurs, classification of people according to their past falls, and prediction of falls that occur after the measurement. Only the third is prognostic. Even among prospective studies, optimism can arise when repeated windows from the same participant enter both training and test sets, when feature selection precedes cross-validation, or when many candidate features are evaluated relative to the number of fallers [@shany;kapoor]. Existing syntheses have either combined retrospective and prospective designs, pooled reconstructed accuracy data across heterogeneous models, or predated the recent expansion of machine learning and computer vision [@howcroft2013;patel;mou;shany]. None has appraised this literature with PROBAST+AI, the 2025 update of the Prediction model Risk Of Bias ASsessment Tool designed for both regression and artificial intelligence (AI) methods [@probast], and none has systematically accounted for repeated publication from the same cohorts."),
('p', "Clinical use of a prediction model requires more than discrimination. It requires a defined target population and index time, a stated prediction horizon, calibrated probabilities, performance that holds in new participants and settings, and evidence that decisions based on the model improve outcomes [@probast;tripodai]. For sensor-based models, transportability depends on the entire measurement pipeline—hardware, placement, acquisition protocol, signal processing, feature construction, and classifier—not the final algorithm alone [@han;probast]."),
('p', "We therefore conducted a systematic review of prognostic models that use wearable- or vision-derived gait measurements to predict future falls in older adults. The objectives were to (1) characterize populations, sensing technologies, gait domains, outcomes, and prediction horizons; (2) distinguish apparent, internally validated, prospectively evaluated, and independently validated performance; (3) evaluate discrimination, calibration, incremental value over clinical predictors, and clinical utility; (4) assess risk of bias and applicability with PROBAST+AI; and (5) judge clinical readiness against explicit criteria and define the evidence required before these models can be deployed. To our knowledge, this is the first review to restrict the evidence to strictly prospective individualized prediction, to cluster overlapping cohorts, to include vision-based as well as wearable sensing, and to apply PROBAST+AI to this field."),

('h1', "2. Materials and Methods"),
('h2', "2.1. Protocol Registration and Reporting"),
('p', "The review protocol was registered in PROSPERO (CRD420261502037). Reporting follows the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses (PRISMA) 2020 statement [@prisma]; the completed PRISMA 2020 checklist is provided in the Supplementary Materials. Prediction-model items were reported according to the TRIPOD-SRMA guidance for systematic reviews of prediction models [@tripodsrma], and data items were based on the CHARMS checklist [@charms]. The review question was as follows: in older adults, how valid, transportable, and clinically ready are models that use wearable- or vision-derived gait measurements, obtained before follow-up, to predict subsequent falls? Deviations from the registered protocol are reported in Section 2.10."),
('h2', "2.2. Eligibility Criteria"),
('p', "Eligibility was defined using the population, index model, comparator, outcome, timing, and setting (PICOTS) framework (Table 1). Eligible reports studied an older-adult population (minimum, mean, or median age ≥60 years, or separately reported older-adult results); quantified gait with a body-worn or portable sensor or a camera-based vision system; completed predictor measurement before the fall-outcome window; ascertained actual subsequent falls; and reported individualized model performance (discrimination, calibration, or classification accuracy). Models could use gait alone or combine gait with clinical predictors, and development, internal validation, temporal testing, updating, and external validation studies were all eligible. Fixed commercial scores were eligible when their inputs included sensor-derived gait and they were evaluated against prospectively ascertained falls."),
('p', "We excluded studies of retrospective or concurrent faller classification, real-time fall detection, prognostic-factor associations without individualized performance, surrogate fall-risk scores as the outcome, measurement-validation studies without future falls, intervention-effect studies, and non-gait sensor models. English-language peer-reviewed journal reports published from 1 January 2000 to 20 July 2026 were considered; the lower limit reflects the introduction of the digital wearable and computer-vision pipelines that are the focus of this review. Reports were never excluded because performance was poor or incompletely reported."),
('table', 'T1'),
('h2', "2.3. Information Sources and Search Strategy"),
('p', "MEDLINE (PubMed), Embase (Ovid), Scopus, Web of Science Core Collection, IEEE Xplore, CINAHL (EBSCOhost), APA PsycINFO, the ACM Digital Library, and the Cochrane Central Register of Controlled Trials (CENTRAL) were searched from 1 January 2000 to 20 July 2026. Google Scholar was searched as a supplementary source, with screening of the first 200 relevance-ranked records. Study and trial registries and ProQuest Dissertations & Theses Global were also searched. Additional sources were backward and forward citation searching of included reports and relevant reviews, the medRxiv and arXiv preprint servers, hand-searching of key journals, and contact with authors to identify further reports. Search strategies combined concept blocks for older adults, gait or walking, wearable, portable, or vision-based sensing, and falls, supplemented by prediction and performance terms; block combinations were adapted to each interface. Full source-specific strategies, limits, dates, and yields are given in Supplementary Table S1."),
('p', "Before the synthesis was finalized, a supplementary known-item check using broader mobility-test terminology (for example, Timed Up and Go, sit-to-stand, and turning) and citation-linked candidates was performed to test the sensitivity of the search. It identified one eligible report that had been excluded at title/abstract screening [@suffoletto26]; this report was reinstated and is included in all analyses."),
('h2', "2.4. Study Selection"),
('p', "Records were deduplicated using digital object identifiers and normalized titles. Titles and abstracts, and then full texts, were assessed against the eligibility criteria. Each record was assessed by one reviewer and every decision was verified by a second reviewer against the source record; all full-text inclusion and exclusion decisions were subsequently cross-checked by the four-member review team, and disagreements were resolved by discussion until consensus was reached. No automation tool excluded records. For each report, the reviewers confirmed from the primary source that gait measurement ended before the fall-outcome window and that participant-level performance was reported. When several exclusion reasons applied, a prespecified hierarchy (publication type, population, index measurement, temporality/outcome, and analysis type) determined the principal reason. Excluded reports that might appear eligible are listed with reasons in Supplementary Table S3."),
('h2', "2.5. Data Extraction and Management of Overlapping Cohorts"),
('p', "A structured form based on CHARMS [@charms] captured bibliographic details, setting, age, sample size and number of fallers, outcome definition and ascertainment, prediction horizon, gait task, device and placement, candidate predictors, modelling method, validation method, discrimination, classification and calibration measures, and principal limitations. Extraction was performed by one reviewer and verified in full by a second reviewer; all extracted values were then cross-checked by the review team, and internal numerical consistency was checked for every structured field. Information not reported was recorded as not reported (NR) and never inferred. When the full text of an included report could not be accessed, data were extracted from the published abstract and other accessible primary material; these reports are flagged in Table 2 and were examined in a sensitivity analysis (Section 2.8)."),
('p', "Participant overlap was assessed from recruitment sites, dates, sample sizes, devices, trial identifiers, author groups, and baseline characteristics. Reports sharing participants were assigned to one cohort family and were not treated as independent replications (Supplementary Table S4). Each performance estimate was classified by validation stage: apparent (performance in the development data), internal validation (participant-level resampling or data splitting), prospective fixed-model evaluation (a previously specified model, rule, or commercial score applied to newly followed participants without refitting), target-sample updating (a model refitted or recalibrated in the evaluation sample), or independent external validation (a fully specified model applied to non-overlapping participants without refitting or reselection of features). Results based on observation-level rather than participant-level data splits were retained only to illustrate data leakage."),
('h2', "2.6. Outcomes and Performance Measures"),
('p', "The primary outcome was any fall occurring after the index measurement. Secondary outcomes were recurrent falls, injurious falls, fall counts, and time to first fall. Prediction horizons were extracted as defined by each report. Discrimination was summarized by the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUC) or concordance statistic (C-index); classification performance by accuracy, sensitivity, specificity, and positive and negative predictive values; calibration by calibration-in-the-large, calibration slope, observed-to-expected ratios, and calibration plots; overall performance by the Brier score; and clinical utility by decision curves and net benefit. Sensitivity, specificity, and predictive values were recalculated only when exact cell counts were published. Apparent, internal, prospective, and external estimates were kept separate throughout."),
('h2', "2.7. Risk of Bias and Applicability"),
('p', "Risk of bias and applicability were assessed with PROBAST+AI [@probast]. Part A assesses concern about the quality of model development and Part B assesses risk of bias in model evaluation; each covers four domains (participants and data sources, predictors, outcome, and analysis) and applicability. Development and evaluation were appraised separately when a report contained both. Signalling questions were answered for the principal gait-containing model of each report, because models within a report usually shared participants, outcomes, and analysis pipelines. Domain judgments were low, high, or unclear, and an overall low judgment required every domain to be low. A high rating required an affirmatively reported design or analysis feature; absence of information was rated unclear. Particular attention was paid to temporal separation of predictors and outcome, participant-level data splitting, handling of repeated windows, nesting of feature selection and tuning within resampling, the number of fallers relative to model complexity, missing data, optimism correction, and calibration. Appraisals were performed by one reviewer, verified by a second, and cross-checked by the review team, with disagreements resolved by consensus. Domain-level judgments and rationales for every report are given in Supplementary Tables S5 and S6. Tools designed for intervention effects (RoB 2 and ROBINS-I) and for prognostic-factor studies (QUIPS) were not applicable."),
('h2', "2.8. Synthesis Methods"),
('p', "Meta-analysis was not appropriate because populations, fall definitions, ascertainment methods, prediction horizons, sensor pipelines, validation stages, and reported metrics were not exchangeable, and several cohorts contributed multiple reports. We therefore performed a structured narrative synthesis organized into five domains: evidence architecture and validation stage; sensing technologies and gait phenotypes; outcomes and horizons; discrimination, incremental value, calibration, and clinical utility; and risk of bias, applicability, and clinical readiness. Findings were interpreted according to validation stage, precision, cohort overlap, and PROBAST+AI judgments rather than by vote counting."),
('p', "To avoid double counting, one representative AUC was selected per cohort family using a predefined hierarchy: (1) an independently evaluated gait-containing model for any fall; otherwise (2) a participant-level internally validated gait-containing model; otherwise (3) the principal apparent gait-containing model. Within a level, the earliest clinically actionable horizon with a matched number of fallers was chosen, and a model with a clinically usable sensitivity–specificity balance was preferred over a numerically higher AUC. Representative AUCs were plotted against the number of participants with the outcome (logarithmic scale) by validation stage, and medians, interquartile ranges (IQRs), and ranges were tabulated overall, by validation stage, and by event-count band (≤15, 16–39, and ≥40 fallers). These summaries are descriptive, unweighted, and not pooled estimates. Robustness was examined in two sensitivity analyses: treating a probably overlapping report as an independent cohort, and repeating the PROBAST+AI summaries after excluding reports appraised without full-text access. Formal tests for small-study effects require a pooled estimate and were not performed; reporting bias was assessed qualitatively from sample size, model multiplicity, selective reporting, and the scarcity of null external evaluations."),
('h2', "2.9. Certainty of Evidence and Clinical Readiness"),
('p', "GRADE guidance for the certainty of discrimination estimates applies to a body of validation studies of the same prognostic model [@grade]. It could not be applied because no locked model had been validated in more than one independent cohort. Clinical readiness was therefore judged against six explicit criteria derived from PROBAST+AI and TRIPOD+AI [@probast;tripodai]: (1) predictors measured before the outcome window; (2) performance estimated beyond the development data; (3) independent external validation of a locked sensor-to-model pipeline; (4) calibration assessed with calibration-in-the-large, slope, and a plot; (5) clinical utility assessed at prespecified thresholds (decision-curve or net-benefit analysis); and (6) low overall risk of bias. Evidence of impact on clinical decisions or patient outcomes was recorded as an additional criterion."),
('h2', "2.10. Deviations from the Registered Protocol"),
('p', "Three analytical elements were specified during analysis rather than in the protocol: the representative-result hierarchy used to select one estimate per cohort family, the descriptive event-count bands, and the sensitivity analysis restricted to reports with full-text access. They were introduced to prevent double counting of overlapping cohorts and to test the robustness of the risk-of-bias findings, and they did not alter eligibility or appraisal decisions."),

('h1', "3. Results"),
('h2', "3.1. Study Selection"),
('p', "The searches identified 14,912 records: 14,667 through databases and registers and 245 through other methods (Supplementary Table S1). After removal of 5433 duplicates, 9479 records were screened and 9127 were excluded. Of 352 reports sought, 11 could not be retrieved and 341 were assessed in full. Of these, 294 were excluded, most commonly because they lacked a prospective fall outcome or valid temporal separation (n = 99), addressed fall detection (n = 53), or reported no individualized performance (n = 44) (Figure 1; Supplementary Table S3). Forty-seven reports representing 37 independent cohort families were included [@marsch11a;marsch11b;greene12;weiss13;schwesig13;doi13;mignardot14;schwenk14;vs15;vs16;howcroft17;drover17;greene18;ihlen18;naitaicha18;howcroft18;bizovska18;marques18;lo19;marques19;gillain19;buisseret20;silva20;sturchio21;bet21;mehdizadeh21;lockhart21;roshdibenam21;greene22;kelly22;dierick22;ma22;dasgupta22;caronni23;horak23;ullrich23;alabiad23;adeli23;shah23;sotirakis24;zhangh24;maiora24;zhangy24;lai25;giardini25;guan25;suffoletto26]."),
('figure', 'F1'),
('h2', "3.2. Study and Model Characteristics"),
('p', "Cohort families ranged from 15 to 1705 participants, and 24 of 37 enrolled fewer than 150 participants (Table 2; full extraction in Supplementary Table S2). Populations included community-dwelling older adults, nursing-home and long-term-care residents, geriatric inpatients and outpatients, emergency-department and post-fracture cohorts, and people with Parkinson disease, dementia, or other neurological disorders. Prediction horizons ranged from 7 days to 5 years, most commonly 6–12 months. Any fall was the dominant outcome; recurrent falls, first falls, and fall counts were modelled less often, and no eligible model predicted injurious falls."),
('p', "Forty-five reports used wearable or portable sensors—IMUs on the trunk, waist, shank, or foot; smartphones; instrumented insoles or footwear; and footswitches—and two linked reports from one dementia cohort used ambient computer vision [@mehdizadeh21;adeli23]. Structured gait tasks predominated, particularly instrumented Timed Up and Go (iTUG) tests and straight walking; free-living monitoring lasted from 24 h to 14 days. Modelling approaches ranged from logistic and survival regression to classification trees, support-vector machines, random forests, and deep neural networks. For three reports, only a separable prospective analysis was eligible: the baseline iTUG model against falls during one month after discharge [@giardini25], the participant-level model of new-onset falls at 18 months [@lo19], and the logistic model of falls over 6 months [@ma22]."),
('table', 'T2'),
('h2', "3.3. Evidence Architecture and Validation Stage"),
('p', "Requiring completion of gait measurement before fall follow-up excluded many studies commonly cited as fall-prediction evidence. The included evidence also contained substantial dependence: seven multi-report cohort families accounted for 17 reports, including four reports from the Fall Risk Assessment in Older Adults (FARAO) cohort [@vs15;vs16;naitaicha18;ihlen18] and three from one Canadian research programme [@howcroft17;drover17;howcroft18] (Supplementary Table S4). Because participant independence could not be established for one of these reports [@drover17], it was grouped with the programme; treating it as independent (38 families) did not change any conclusion. Counting each publication as an independent replication would have overstated the breadth of evidence."),
('p', "Most estimates came from the development data. Seventeen reports reported apparent performance only (15 cohort families had no estimate beyond apparent performance), 24 reported internal validation, five reported prospective evaluation of a fixed model or score or target-sample updating, and one reported independent external validation. Internal validation did not guarantee unbiased estimates: feature selection, feature engineering, or hyperparameter tuning were often performed on the full dataset before cross-validation. The consequence was demonstrated directly in one deep-learning study, in which splitting repeated walking samples rather than participants produced an AUC of 0.94, whereas participant-level evaluation produced AUCs of 0.65–0.75 [@naitaicha18]. Similar non-nested selection affected other internally validated models [@ihlen18;howcroft18;zhangh24;guan25]."),
('h2', "3.4. Sensing Technologies and Gait Phenotypes"),
('p', "Structured tests captured iTUG phases, turning, postural transitions, straight walking, and dynamic stability, whereas free-living monitoring captured walking quantity, bout structure, pace, rhythm, variability, smoothness, symmetry, harmonicity, and complexity. The most consistently informative domains were not mean gait speed alone but gait variability, trunk stability and harmonicity, turning, and the distribution of daily walking bouts [@doi13;vs15;bizovska18;shah23;caronni23]. Nominally similar features were, however, computed with different devices, axes, filters, bout definitions, and aggregation windows, so they were not directly equivalent across studies."),
('p', "Ambient computer vision was evaluated in a single long-term-care dementia cohort. Frequently updated vision-derived gait features improved the AUC for a fall within the next 4 weeks from 0.562 without gait to 0.762 with gait (sensitivity 72.8%, specificity 73.2%) [@adeli23], and an earlier report from the same cohort reported AUCs of 0.80 at 7 days and 0.67 at 30 days [@mehdizadeh21]. These results are promising for continuous, device-free risk estimation but derive from one site and require independent validation across camera environments and populations."),
('h2', "3.5. Outcomes and Prediction Horizons"),
('p', "Horizons ranged from 7–30 days in dementia care [@mehdizadeh21;adeli23] to 3–12 months in most community and clinical cohorts and 24–60 months in one Parkinson disease cohort [@sotirakis24]. These tasks differ in baseline risk, intervention opportunity, and the acceptable balance of false-positive and false-negative predictions. Fall ascertainment ranged from daily calendars with regular telephone contact to single recall interviews at 12–24 months [@marsch11a;greene12] and facility records. Where a first-fall outcome was modelled over time, discrimination attenuated with longer horizons and was absent beyond 12 months [@mignardot14]. In the only fall-count model, fitted in 15 people with Parkinson disease, explained variance changed from 0.50 to 0.73 after exclusion of participants with more than 10 falls, including one participant who reported 127 falls [@greene22]."),
('h2', "3.6. Discrimination Across Validation Stages"),
('p', "A representative AUC matched to the number of fallers was available for 24 of 37 cohort families. The median was 0.761 (IQR 0.715–0.841; range 0.505–0.990) (Table 3). Estimates declined across validation stages, from a median of 0.771 for apparent performance and 0.762 for internal validation to 0.688 for the only prospectively evaluated fixed score [@lai25] and 0.530 for the only independent external validation [@zhangy24]. By event-count band, medians were 0.870 for ≤15 fallers, 0.767 for 16–39 fallers, and 0.720 for ≥40 fallers (Figure 2)."),
('p', "The highest values came from very small, flexibly modelled samples: an emergency-department neural network reported a leave-one-out AUC of 0.99 with 14 fallers [@dasgupta22], a post-fracture model an apparent AUC of 0.91 with approximately 10 fallers [@giardini25], and a Parkinson disease model an apparent AUC of 0.94 after best-subset selection from 80 candidate measures in 34 participants [@shah23]. Larger or more stringently evaluated studies produced modest or near-chance discrimination: AUCs of 0.495–0.505 for multisensor fusion in 281 community-dwelling adults [@silva20], 0.56 for a secondary prospective analysis in a geriatric clinic [@roshdibenam21], 0.67 in repeated hold-out testing of step-based parameters [@alabiad23], and a sensitivity of 0.61 with specificity of 0.66 in a held-out test set from a 1705-participant cohort [@kelly22]."),
('table', 'T3'),
('figure', 'F2'),
('h2', "3.7. Incremental Value over Clinical Information"),
('p', "Several reports compared gait-containing models with clinical information in the same participants. Daily-life accelerometry increased the apparent AUC from 0.68 for questionnaires and clinical tests to 0.82 in community-dwelling adults [@vs15]; instrumented stand-and-walk measures increased it from 0.685 for age and fall history to 0.751 in older men [@horak23]; selected daily-life gait and turning features increased it from 0.77 for fall history to 0.94 in Parkinson disease [@shah23]; and in neurological rehabilitation, adding Timed Up and Go turning duration to fall history increased the AUC only from 0.66 to 0.69, the same gain as a clinical balance scale [@caronni23]. In the largest emergency-department cohort (360 participants; 94 fallers), adding four IMU-derived features to nine clinical predictors increased the AUC from 0.67 to 0.72 (*p* = 0.19) and, at a prespecified 30% risk threshold, sensitivity from 0.45 to 0.57 and specificity from 0.76 to 0.80 [@suffoletto26]. All these increments were estimated in the development data without optimism correction."),
('h2', "3.8. Calibration, Clinical Utility, and Independent Validation"),
('p', "Calibration-related information was reported in 5 of 47 reports: Brier scores in two [@marsch11a;marsch11b], Hosmer–Lemeshow tests in one [@vs15], a qualitative comparison of predicted and observed survival in one [@mehdizadeh21], and calibration intercepts and slopes in one [@zhangy24]. No report presented a calibration plot, and none reported decision-curve analysis, net benefit, or the effect of model use on clinical decisions or fall rates. Operating points were frequently unbalanced: a deep-learning model with an AUC of 0.78 had a sensitivity of 0.33 at its best-AUC setting [@maiora24], a commercial score achieved a sensitivity of 1.00 but a specificity of 0.375 and positive predictive value of 0.20 [@lai25], and a sensor-supplemented Timed Up and Go rule had a specificity of 95.9% but a sensitivity of 29.2% [@dierick22]."),
('p', "The single independent external validation applied three published questionnaire and trunk-accelerometry models to 263 new participants, of whom 105 fell [@zhangy24]. Gait-containing models achieved AUCs of 0.49–0.59, sensitivities of 0.32–0.55, and specificities of 0.45–0.89, and a questionnaire-only comparator performed at least as well (AUC up to 0.68). The published models were miscalibrated in the new cohort (for example, calibration intercept −0.83 and slope 1.40 for the combined model for all falls), and recalibration or updating recovered little discrimination. Predictor processing, however, differed from the original development, so this study tests transportability of the published models rather than of their exact pipelines. A separate prospective blind test of a random-forest classifier trained on fall history reported an accuracy of 81.6% but included only 44 participants and 9 fallers [@lockhart21]. No model had been validated in more than one independent cohort."),
('h2', "3.9. Risk of Bias and Applicability"),
('p', "All 45 development assessments (PROBAST+AI Part A) were at high overall concern, and all 47 evaluation assessments (Part B) were at high overall risk of bias (Figure 3; Supplementary Table S5). The analysis domain drove these judgments: it was rated high in 45 of 45 development and 46 of 47 evaluation assessments. Other domains were more often low: in Part B, participants and data sources were rated low in 19, unclear in 11, and high in 17 reports; predictors low in 37, unclear in 8, and high in 2; and outcome low in 33, unclear in 9, and high in 5. The only evaluation with a low-risk analysis domain was rated high overall because predictor reconstruction differed from the development specification [@zhangy24]. Recurrent analysis problems were too few fallers relative to the number of candidate predictors, non-nested feature selection and tuning, performance estimated in the development data, complete-case analysis, and absent optimism correction and calibration. When the 11 reports appraised without full-text access were excluded, all 34 remaining development assessments and all 36 evaluation assessments remained at high overall risk of bias."),
('p', "Applicability concern was high overall in 31 of 45 development and 32 of 47 evaluation assessments (Supplementary Table S6), mainly because participants were drawn from selected populations (for example, a single disease group, one facility, or men only). Predictor and outcome applicability were usually of low concern."),
('figure', 'F3'),
('h2', "3.10. Clinical Readiness"),
('p', "Table 4 summarizes the evidence against the predefined readiness criteria. All reports measured gait before the outcome window, and 30 of 47 reports (22 of 37 cohort families) estimated performance beyond the development data. However, only one report performed independent external validation, one reported calibration intercepts and slopes, none presented a calibration plot or decision-curve analysis, none was at low risk of bias, and none evaluated clinical impact. Reporting bias was likely: most small studies compared multiple features, classifiers, or thresholds while reporting incomplete uncertainty, and null external evaluations were rare."),
('table', 'T4'),

('h1', "4. Discussion"),
('h2', "4.1. Principal Findings"),
('p', "This systematic review of 47 reports from 37 cohort families shows that wearable- and vision-derived gait measures carry a genuine prospective signal for future falls, but that no model is ready for routine clinical use. The signal recurred across independent cohorts and concerned biologically coherent domains—gait variability, trunk stability, turning, and the structure of daily walking—and several models added discrimination to fall history or brief clinical tests. The central finding, however, is a validity gradient rather than a single accuracy estimate: discrimination was highest in small samples evaluated in their own development data and lowest where evaluation was most rigorous. The only independent external validation yielded AUCs of 0.49–0.59, calibration was almost never assessed, clinical utility was never assessed, and every evaluation was at high risk of bias."),
('h2', "4.2. Comparison with Previous Reviews"),
('p', "Earlier reviews documented the promise of inertial sensors for characterizing fallers and warned that small samples and flexible modelling inflated reported accuracy [@howcroft2013;patel;shany]. A 2026 meta-analysis of 20 wearable studies reported a summary AUC of 0.85, pooled sensitivity of 0.55, and specificity of 0.89 [@mou]. That synthesis combined prospective and retrospective designs and reconstructed 2 × 2 accuracy data across heterogeneous models, answering a broader diagnostic-accuracy question. By restricting eligibility to strictly prospective, individualized prediction, clustering overlapping cohorts, and separating validation stages, the present review shows why such pooled estimates are unlikely to transfer: the higher values are concentrated in apparent and internally validated estimates, whereas independent evaluation performed close to chance. Our findings are therefore complementary and explain the gap between reported accuracy and clinical uptake."),
('h2', "4.3. Why Reported Accuracy Overstates Clinical Readiness"),
('p', "Four mechanisms account for most of the optimism. First, events were sparse: 24 of 37 cohort families enrolled fewer than 150 participants, and seven representative estimates were based on 15 or fewer fallers, far below the sample sizes required for stable model development [@riley2020]. Second, information leaked between training and test data when repeated windows from one participant were split across partitions or when features were selected on the full dataset before resampling [@kapoor]; one study quantified an inflation from 0.65–0.75 to 0.94 [@naitaicha18]. Third, calibration was ignored, although a well-discriminating model can still systematically over- or underestimate risk, which is the property that determines whether a threshold-based decision is appropriate [@vancalster]. Fourth, no study linked predictions to a clinical action, so net benefit at plausible thresholds remains unknown [@vickers]. These problems are consequential rather than procedural, and they are all correctable in future studies."),
('h2', "4.4. Technologies, Gait Phenotypes, and Outcomes"),
('p', "Wearables supported two complementary strategies. Structured instrumented tests quantify turning, transitions, and dynamic stability during a standardized encounter and are suited to rapid triage in clinics or emergency departments [@greene12;caronni23;suffoletto26]. Free-living monitoring captures habitual exposure, variability, and day-to-day walking organization and may suit ongoing surveillance [@vs15;kelly22;shah23]. Neither strategy was consistently superior. Free-living walking quantity is also ambiguous, because more walking may indicate greater capacity but also more exposure to fall opportunities; models should separate vulnerability from exposure rather than treat all activity features as risk markers. Ambient vision avoids device burden and enables frequently updated risk estimates, but all prospective evidence came from one dementia facility [@mehdizadeh21;adeli23]. Transfer will require validation of both the measurement layer and the prognostic model, because camera geometry, lighting, occlusion, walking aids, and software versions can alter predictors before any classifier is applied. Finally, predicting a fall within the next week in long-term care, a fall within 6 months after emergency discharge, and a fall within 5 years in Parkinson disease are different clinical tasks that warrant different thresholds and separate validation."),
('h2', "4.5. Implications for Clinical Practice"),
('p', "Sensor-derived gait measures should currently complement, not replace, guideline-recommended multifactorial fall-risk assessment [@montero]. Until a locked model has been independently validated and calibrated in the intended setting, gait-derived risk estimates should not be used alone to allocate or withhold fall-prevention interventions. They may reasonably prompt a fuller clinical assessment or be used in research. Clinicians and health systems evaluating commercial fall-risk devices should ask for evidence of independent external validation, calibration, and performance at the intended decision threshold, rather than development-sample accuracy or AUC alone."),
('h2', "4.6. Priorities for Future Research"),
('p', "Table 5 translates the gaps identified in this review into minimum standards for future studies. The immediate priority is not a further development study in a small convenience sample but independent, adequately powered, multisite validation of existing locked models and locked signal-processing pipelines [@riley2024], reported according to TRIPOD+AI [@tripodai]. Injurious-fall prediction is a distinct unmet need: no eligible model addressed injurious falls despite their direct relevance to hospitalization and loss of independence. Studies should use consensus fall definitions and prospective daily calendars with regular follow-up [@lamb], account for death and other competing events, and compare gait-containing models with a parsimonious clinical model. Only after transportability has been demonstrated should impact studies test whether model-guided care improves decisions and reduces falls."),
('table', 'T5'),
('h2', "4.7. Strengths and Limitations"),
('p', "This review has several strengths. It used a strict definition of prospective prediction, excluded retrospective classification and association-only evidence, searched clinical and engineering databases, clustered overlapping publications, separated development from evaluation, and applied PROBAST+AI. It preserved poor and null results rather than pooling non-exchangeable estimates, and it judged clinical readiness against explicit criteria."),
('p', "Some limitations should be considered. First, the search strategies required gait terminology in several sources; the known-item check showed that mobility-test terminology can evade such searches, and one additional report was identified and included. Restriction to English-language, peer-reviewed journal reports may have introduced language and publication bias. Second, screening, extraction, and appraisal used a verification design, in which each decision made by one reviewer was checked by a second reviewer and cross-checked by the review team, rather than fully independent duplicate assessment; inter-rater agreement statistics were therefore not calculated. Third, full texts were unavailable for 11 included reports, for which extraction relied on the published abstract and accessible primary material; unreported items were recorded as not reported, and the sensitivity analysis excluding these reports did not change any risk-of-bias conclusion. Fourth, PROBAST+AI judgments refer to the principal gait-containing model of each report rather than to every model variant. Fifth, a representative AUC was available for 24 of 37 cohort families, and families without one were generally less completely reported; the descriptive gradient may therefore be affected by reporting completeness, and the representative-result hierarchy involved reviewer judgment. Finally, heterogeneity precluded meta-analysis, and selective reporting could not be quantified."),

('h1', "5. Conclusions"),
('p', "Wearable- and vision-derived gait measures contain a genuine prognostic signal for future falls in older adults, particularly through gait variability, trunk stability, turning, and daily walking structure. However, the published models are not yet ready for clinical deployment: every evaluation was at high risk of bias, calibration and clinical utility were almost never assessed, and the only independent external validation performed close to chance. Progress now depends less on new algorithms than on independent, adequately powered, multisite validation of locked sensor-to-model pipelines, with calibration, prespecified decision thresholds, net-benefit analysis, and subsequent impact evaluation. These standards provide a clear roadmap for moving digital gait biomarkers from promising signal to trustworthy clinical tool."),
]

